# React Hooks Rules

## Rules of Hooks - Critical Constraint

**NEVER call hooks conditionally or after early returns.**

### The Problem

React hooks (useState, useEffect, useMemo, useCallback, etc.) must be called in the same order on every render. If you call hooks after a conditional return or inside a conditional block, you violate the Rules of Hooks.

### ❌ WRONG - Hooks After Early Return

```typescript
function Component() {
  const [state, setState] = useState(0)
  
  if (someCondition) {
    return <div>Early return</div>
  }
  
  // ❌ BAD: Hook called after conditional return
  const memoized = useMemo(() => compute(), [deps])
  
  return <div>...</div>
}
```

### ✅ CORRECT - All Hooks Before Any Returns

```typescript
function Component() {
  const [state, setState] = useState(0)
  
  // ✅ GOOD: All hooks called before any conditional returns
  const memoized = useMemo(() => compute(), [deps])
  
  if (someCondition) {
    return <div>Early return</div>
  }
  
  return <div>...</div>
}
```

### Common Patterns to Avoid

1. **Early returns before hooks:**
   ```typescript
   // ❌ BAD
   if (!mounted) return null
   const value = useMemo(...)
   ```

2. **Hooks inside conditionals:**
   ```typescript
   // ❌ BAD
   if (condition) {
     const value = useMemo(...)
   }
   ```

3. **Hooks after guard clauses:**
   ```typescript
   // ❌ BAD
   if (!data) return <Loading />
   const processed = useMemo(() => process(data), [data])
   ```

### Best Practice

**Always structure components so ALL hooks are called at the top, before any conditional logic or early returns.**

```typescript
function Component({ data, mounted }) {
  // 1. All useState hooks
  const [state, setState] = useState(0)
  
  // 2. All useMemo/useCallback hooks
  const memoized = useMemo(() => compute(), [deps])
  
  // 3. All useEffect hooks
  useEffect(() => {
    // ...
  }, [deps])
  
  // 4. THEN conditional logic and early returns
  if (!mounted) return null
  if (!data) return <Loading />
  
  // 5. Finally, the main return
  return <div>...</div>
}
```

### When You Need Conditional Hook Behavior

If you need conditional behavior, handle it INSIDE the hook, not by conditionally calling the hook:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Conditional logic inside the hook
useEffect(() => {
  if (!mounted) return
  // do something
}, [mounted])

// ✅ GOOD: Early return inside useMemo
const value = useMemo(() => {
  if (!data) return null
  return compute(data)
}, [data])
```
